{"ts": 1789701731.761029, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "This confirms my suspicion: these three features (2, 21, 35) are essentially near-perfect predictors in-sample, and combining them boosts the Sharpe even further (up to 73.5). This is a classic sign of either:\n\n1. **Data leakage** \u2014 these features may be constructed using information that includes/looks ahead into returns (e.g., they could literally be derived from the target itself with some noise), or\n2. **In-sample overfitting artifacts** specific to this dataset that won't hold out-of-sample.\n\nA genuine daily-return equity factor with annualized Sharpe of 50-70 is essentially impossible in real markets (that would imply near-arbitrage). This is far outside plausible ranges for real risk premia (typical good factors have Sharpe 0.5-2.0). Such extreme in-sample Sharpes usually indicate the feature is a transformation of the realized return itself (leakage), which means out-of-sample (truly future, unseen) performance would collapse to near zero or even be unstable/negative since the \"leakage\" mechanism won't hold up.\n\nGiven the task explicitly asks for **out-of-sample** Sharpe prediction, I should be very cautious about trusting these extreme values. Let me test a bit more to understand \u2014 check if these features individually are stable, and also check reasonable combinations of the more modest, plausible features (like 10, 14, 3, 29) which have believable Sharpes."}
